Hi r.g.r.n people. Long time lurker first time poster. Be gentle.

Just thought I'd share some musings I had recently in an underground
speleological snafu. I went caving with some friends and became
geographically embarassed - lost, waited, and eventually rescued by
the emergency services. And it was fantastic.Stalling in Gauntlet made
exits appear, and the same isn't completely untrue of caving. Just Let
Someone Know Before You Go, kids.

I knew I was in for a good time when I saw a yabbie, a local miniature
crayfish-like critter, on the ground just as I entered the caverns. A
grid bug. The RNG favours me.

 OOO
OOOOO
OO@OO
 OOO

- "Oh shit."

We scrambled over and under boulders for what seemed like an eternity
- gaily following our intrepid archaeologist - I thought they //knew//
caves! - and that was it for the whole time down there. Imagine a
dungeon created solely of boulders - nudity and squeezing required at
nearly every step.

That was the gist of it - luckily I had the fit and feisty Elvy-val-
hum-fem-law with me to inspire acts of reckless chivalry, restore HP
with and to tacitly remind me to keep my own whinging and complaining
hidden away. We didn't really need time to stand still as we lied in
each other's arms - we wanted the rescue party there /yesterday/.

It occurred to me that the mazes in NH need to be threedimensional -
less X and Y but with more Z. Trapdoors, stairs, only small rooms
encountered each time, to really drive home the way in which one can
become lost ... we could have used a pick-axe at one stage, although
digging upwards isn't really that sensible in the Real World(tm). 90
degree level flips would also create havoc - if the player becomes
lost or passes out, why would they remember which way was N or S ?
this would limit the game to square levels, I realise.

Why did we head down into the Mazes without adequate equipment? Well,
I guess we didn't plan on getting lost. "This liquorice tastes
terrible!" "You feel a strange sensation for a moment, but it passes"
doesn't mean something magical just happened. It might just mean your
character has realised they're /lost/.

We had the occasional meeting with small luminescent creatures - and I
immediately thought that that was something missing from the walls of
the dungeon. You could eat them - but then you lose their light! Roots
of trees and other plant life, edible to various degrees, from the
surface, could also grace the upper levels.

Another suppressed giggle I had was that the caves were occasionally
spotted with graffiti - nothing as profound as 'sum quod eris' but
monosyllabic observations like 'DOG' and 'Wannie Walker'. Arrows and
reflective dots and what not added to the confusion.

Playing NH might not make you into an instant caver - but its memory
will keep you in good spirits until the cavalry arrive. And they do
have such spiffy equipment - although I knew it would be rather poor
form to roll(1) my rescuer for his headtorch! :-)

Alex

